:name: Keychron Q3 Max
:description: Renode emulator for Keychron Q3 Max QMK firmware (STM32F401 + LKBT51)

# ELF path can be overridden via:
#   renode -e '$bin=@/path/to/firmware.elf; include @emulator/renode/q3_max.resc'
$bin?=@.build/keychron_q3_max_ansi_encoder_keychron.elf

using sysbus
mach create "q3_max"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @emulator/renode/q3_max.repl

# USART2 is QMK's CONSOLE UART when CONSOLE_ENABLE=yes is set in the
# emu-diagnostic build. Open the analyzer window unconditionally — it
# is harmless if the stock build is silent.
showAnalyzer sysbus.usart2

macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadELF $bin

    # F401CC OTP: F_SIZE half-word at 0x1FFF7A22 reports flash size in KB.
    # F401CC = 256 KB → 0x0100. Without this, ChibiOS efl_lld_init() leaves
    # the flash descriptor unset and backing_store_init() panics.
    sysbus WriteWord 0x1FFF7A22 0x0100

    # Patch out chSysPolledDelayX (0x08025184). ChibiOS busy-loops on
    # DWT CYCCNT (at 0xE0001004) for short delays; Renode's Cortex-M
    # model does not implement DWT, so reads return 0 and the loop
    # spins forever. We replace the first instruction with `bx lr` so
    # the delay returns immediately. Accuracy is fine: callers tolerate
    # over-fast delays (it is a minimum, not a fixed sleep).
    sysbus WriteWord 0x08025184 0x4770

    # Patch out spiSend (0x08025e2e) and spiExchange (0x08025e0c).
    # ChibiOS SPI HAL kicks off DMA-driven transfers then blocks the
    # calling thread on a binary semaphore signalled by the DMA TC ISR.
    # Renode's DMA model does not interact with our SPI peripheral to
    # generate completion IRQs, so the thread sleeps forever. We make
    # both functions `movs r0, #0; bx lr` — they return success with
    # no data transferred. The Q3 Max's SPI-driven SNLED27351 LED
    # driver writes silently fail (acceptable; RGB rendering is out
    # of scope for v1.0). The LKBT51 wireless co-MCU stub will be
    # attached in Phase 2 via a different Renode peripheral path
    # that does not depend on ChibiOS SPI driver semantics.
    sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x08025e2e 0x47702000
    sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x08025e0c 0x47702000

    # Patch out rtc_enter_init (0x08027b54) and rtc_exit_init.
    # ChibiOS RTC HAL initialises the calendar by setting RTC_ISR.INIT
    # (bit 7) then polling RTC_ISR.INITF (bit 6) until the chip
    # acknowledges. We replaced Renode's STM32F4_RTC peripheral with
    # plain memory (the real model crashes on DateRegister=0 writes
    # the firmware emits), so INITF never asserts and the poll spins
    # forever. The Q3 Max uses RTC only for sleep timestamps which
    # are not exercised in v1.0. Make rtc_enter_init a no-op.
    sysbus WriteWord 0x08027b54 0x4770
"""
runMacro $reset

# Workaround: Renode's NVIC model returns RETTOBASE (ICSR bit 11) = 0
# unconditionally. ChibiOS port-armv7m's __port_irq_epilogue tests this
# bit to decide whether to run the preemption check on ISR exit. With
# bit 11 stuck at 0, ChibiOS skips the preemption check and sleeping
# threads are never resumed — boot hangs in the first chThdSleep.
#
# We patch the bit on every ICSR read. RETTOBASE = 1 means "no preempted
# active exceptions"; that is the correct value when ChibiOS reads ICSR
# from the IRQ epilogue of a single, non-nested IRQ — which is the only
# case ChibiOS reads ICSR for. Setting it unconditionally is safe here.
sysbus SetHookAfterPeripheralRead sysbus.nvic """
if offset == 0xD04:
    value = value | 0x800
"""
